NAME
    hwcount - host instructions, cycles and IPC of a program's window

SYNOPSIS
    hwcount --from TEXT [--to TEXT] [--per N] [--units FILE]
            [--functions N] [--period N] -- <program> [args]

DESCRIPTION
    A development tool of the emusen.dev module, Linux only. It runs
    <program>, watches its standard error, and counts between the first
    line that holds --from and the first after it that holds --to, or
    the program's end. Time before the window, a state being loaded for
    instance, is not counted. The program's standard error passes through
    to hwcount's own, and its standard output follows the report.

    The report is plain rows, one fact a line, for grep and awk:

        window seconds S per N ms_each M
        thread TID NAME instr I cycles C ipc R branches B misses M
        sampled thread TID samples N lost L
        unit instr I cycles C ipc R share P% NAME
        function instr I cycles C ipc R share P% NAME
        exit STATUS

    A thread row is a count: a group of four hardware events (cycles,
    instructions, branches, branch misses), user mode only, opened on
    every thread alive when --from was seen. A thread started later is
    not counted. --per N divides every count and gives ms_each, so
    --per 200 over 200 fields prints figures a field.

    With --units FILE the program's first thread is sampled as well,
    once every --period (default 1,000,000) cycles and once every --period
    instructions, and each address is taken to a place: a symbol of the
    program (nm -n --defined-only -C), a row of /tmp/perf-<pid>.map for
    compiled code, 'compiled ?' for anonymous memory without a row, or a
    library's file name. The file gives a unit to a place, one line
    'pattern  unit', two spaces between them, # for comments; the first
    pattern found in the place's name wins, and a place no pattern finds
    is 'compiled' or 'other'. A unit row's counts are its share of
    samples times that thread's counted total, so they are estimates,
    with an error of about the square root of the row's samples, and its
    IPC is the ratio of its samples. --functions N adds the N largest
    places.

    A program started through 'systemd-run --scope' keeps its process, so
    a capped scope may stand between hwcount and the program. The map
    /tmp/perf-<pid>.map is removed at the end. The exit status is 2 when
    the command could not run, the window never opened or the kernel
    refused the counters, and otherwise 0, with the program's own status
    in the exit row.

    The counters are perf_event_open(2)'s generic hardware events, so the
    rows mean the same on any host the kernel names them for. On AMD Zen
    4, instructions is event C0h and cycles 76h. The two samplers and the
    group need six counters, which that core has; a smaller core
    multiplexes, and the group's totals are scaled by the time it ran.

    Sapphire's table of units is its examples/scripts/units.txt.

EXAMPLES
    The arena's window, a field's figures on every thread and by unit:

        dianash -c 'hwcount --from "frame 9100 at" --to "frame 9300 at"
            --per 200 --units units.txt --functions 40 -- bench game ...'

    Only the threads' rows:

        dianash -c 'hwcount --from start --to end -- prog' | grep '^thread'
